To build a proprietary data management and visualization software, it’s crucial to approach it with a solid data strategy and architectural foundation. 
You're already thinking in the right direction with compartmentalization, pipeline, and coupling-decoupling. Here's a broader list of key data aspects you should take care of during the planning and development phases:


 Core Aspects to Consider:

    1. Data Compartmentalization & Modeling

        Modular Architecture: Organize data into well-defined domains (e.g., content, user behavior, financials, operations).
        Schema Definition: Clearly define data schemas and entity relationships.
        Operational vs Analytical Data: Separate the two for better performance and manageability.
        Role-Based Access: Implement access controls based on user roles and data compartments.

    2. Data Pipeline Design
    
        ETL/ELT Framework: Design a robust, scalable extraction, transformation, and loading process.
        Data Ingestion: Support both real-time and batch ingestion from APIs, third-party sources, and databases.
        Data Quality Checks: Implement validation, cleansing, and deduplication at each stage.
        Version Control: Maintain version histories for pipelines and transformations.
    
    3. Coupling-Decoupling Principles
    
        Layered Architecture: Ensure ingestion, processing, storage, and visualization layers are loosely coupled.
        Staging Areas: Use intermediary zones like data lakes for better modularity.
        API-Driven Communication: Facilitate interoperability between modules using APIs.
      
    4. Data Standardization & Governance
    
        Data Dictionary: Maintain a centralized repository of definitions and metadata.
        Metadata Tagging: Tag data assets for better lineage tracking.
        Standard Formats: Define consistent naming conventions, units, and data formats.

Architectural & Structural Considerations:

    5. Data Storage Strategy
    
        Storage Selection: Choose between data lake, data warehouse, or lakehouse depending on use case.
        Scalability: Ensure storage solutions can grow with increasing data volumes.
        Partitioning & Indexing: Optimize for query performance.
        Retention Policies: Implement archiving, purging, and data lifecycle management.
      
    6. Data Security & Access Control
    
        Encryption: Protect data both at rest and in transit.
        Authentication & Authorization: Use standards like OAuth and RBAC.
        Audit Trails: Log data access and changes.
        Compliance: Ensure alignment with regulations like GDPR, HIPAA, etc.

Visualization Layer Considerations:

    7. Semantic Layer Design
    
        Metric Abstraction: Define KPIs, calculated metrics, and hierarchies independent of raw data.
        Reusability: Create a plug-and-play layer for visualization tools.
        Self-Service Analytics: Empower users with easy access to key metrics.
    
    8. Real-Time vs Aggregated Views
    
        Use-Case Mapping: Determine which data needs real-time processing versus batch aggregation.
        Time-Series Analysis: Enable period-over-period comparisons (MoM, QoQ, YoY).
    
    9. Performance & Scalability
    
        Pre-Aggregation: Use materialized views and summary tables.
        Caching Mechanism: Implement caching for high-usage queries.
        Distributed Processing: Use scalable computing solutions.
    
    10. Modular Visualization Engine
    
        Widget-Based Design: Build reusable components for charts, filters, and tables.
        Customization: Allow for theming and user-defined views.
        Interactivity: Support drill-downs, filters, exports, and dynamic controls.

User Experience & Collaboration

    11. Data Collaboration & Annotation
    
        Collaboration Tools: Enable tagging, commenting, and alerting.
        Sharing Capabilities: Integrate sharing features for reports and dashboards.
      
    12. Self-Service Analytics Enablement
    
        Guided Exploration: Provide intuitive UI/UX for non-technical users.
        NLP Search: Allow natural language querying and prompt-based insights.

Other Strategic Aspects

    13. Strategic Planning Add-ons
    
        Data Lineage & Observability: Track end-to-end data flow.
        Error Handling: Set up automatic alerts for pipeline failures or schema drifts.
        Comprehensive Documentation: Maintain white papers and change logs for all modules.
